=== Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "C", dense: true)[
  The nephron is the microscopic tube in which urine is formed; each kidney has about a million.
]

#sol-item(2, "A", dense: true)[
  The liver deaminates excess amino acids and converts the nitrogen to urea.
]

#sol-item(3, "B", dense: true)[
  Useful substances are selectively reabsorbed; the glucose is taken back completely.
]

#sol-item(4, "C", dense: true)[
  Homeostasis keeps temperature, water, glucose, pH and salts steady despite external change.
]

#sol-item(5, "D", dense: true)[
  The kidney removes urea and other metabolic waste; the others are digestive.
]

#sol-item(6, "A", dense: true)[
  High hydrostatic pressure in the glomerular capillaries forces small molecules out.
]

#sol-item(7, "D", dense: true)[
  Glucose is reabsorbed completely; its presence suggests diabetes mellitus.
]

#sol-item(8, "A", dense: true)[
  Evaporation of sweat cools the body, and sweat carries away water, salts and some urea.
]

#sol-item(9, "B", dense: true)[
  Constriction keeps warm blood away from the surface, conserving heat.
]

#sol-item(10, "D", dense: true)[
  ADH increases the permeability of the collecting duct to water, so more water is reabsorbed.
]

#sol-item(11, "D", dense: true)[
  Carbon dioxide is produced by respiration and leaves in exhaled air.
]

#sol-item(12, "A", dense: true)[
  Less ADH is released, so less water is reabsorbed and the urine is dilute and plentiful.
]

#sol-item(13, "B", dense: true)[
  By varying the amount of water reabsorbed, the kidney keeps the blood concentration constant.
]

#sol-item(14, "B", dense: true)[
  Without ADH the kidneys cannot reabsorb enough water, so large volumes of dilute urine are produced.
]

#sol-item(15, "C", dense: true)[
  Deamination produces urea, and the breakdown of haemoglobin produces bile pigments.
]

#sol-item(16, "A", dense: true)[
  Filtration removes urea from the blood into the urine.
]

#sol-item(17, "D", dense: true)[
  The proximal tubule reabsorbs glucose and amino acids by active transport.
]

#sol-item(18, "C", dense: true)[
  Evaporation requires heat, which is taken from the skin.
]

#sol-item(19, "D", dense: true)[
  The bladder stores urine; the ureter carries it from the kidney and the urethra carries it out.
]

#sol-item(20, "A", dense: true)[
  Dialysis uses a selectively permeable membrane to remove urea and excess salts from the blood.
]

#sol-item(21, "B", dense: true)[
  Urine is produced by the kidneys; the skin does not make it.
]

#sol-item(22, "B", dense: true)[
  Rapid contraction requires respiration, whose by-product is heat.
]

#sol-item(23, "C", dense: true)[
  The loop of Henle creates the osmotic gradient that allows water to be reabsorbed from the collecting duct.
]

#sol-item(24, "D", dense: true)[
  Insulin causes cells to take up glucose and the liver to store it as glycogen.
]

#sol-item(25, "A", dense: true)[
  Faeces are unabsorbed food, so removing them is egestion; urea is a metabolic waste, so removing it is excretion.
]

#sol-item(26, "C", dense: true)[
  The wider vessel entering and narrower vessel leaving maintain a high hydrostatic pressure for filtration.
]

#sol-item(27, "D", dense: true)[
  The amino group is removed and converted to urea; the remainder can be used for energy.
]

#sol-item(28, "C", dense: true)[
  The hypothalamus coordinates the responses, so a failure means the cooling responses are not switched on.
]

#sol-item(29, "B", dense: true)[
  Sweat contains metabolic waste as well as water.
]

#sol-item(30, "D", dense: true)[
  Negative feedback reverses the original change, restoring the steady state.
]

